  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does the coil in a relay do?",
      "a": "The coil is a small electromagnet. A small control current through it makes a magnetic field that pulls on an iron arm nearby.",
      "asks": [
        "How is a relay switched on?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the armature in a relay?",
      "a": "The armature is a pivoting iron arm. When the coil pulls it, it swings over and pushes two metal contacts together, closing the second circuit.",
      "asks": [
        "What moves inside a relay?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a relay have a spring?",
      "a": "When the control current stops, the coil lets go and the spring pulls the armature back, opening the contacts. That is why a relay clicks off as well as on.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a relay switch off?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are the two circuits in a relay kept separate?",
      "a": "The control circuit and the power circuit are joined only by magnetism, not by wire. So a delicate low-voltage circuit can safely switch a high-voltage or high-current one.",
      "asks": [
        "Is a relay safe for switching mains?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where are relays used?",
      "a": "In cars to switch the starter motor and headlights, in washing machines, in traffic lights and in home automation, wherever a small signal must switch a big load. You can often hear them click.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the clicking sound in a car?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Konrad Zuse's Z3 computer of 1941 was built from about 2,600 relays.",
    "In a relay, the control circuit and the switched circuit are linked only by magnetism."
  ],
